<Include xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       What the bundle hands whichever MSI runs. Included into BOTH MsiPackage elements.

       The two packages cannot be factored into one - they have different SourceFiles and complementary
       InstallConditions, and Burn has no loop over a chain - but their contents can, and had to be. Written
       out per package, adding or renaming a setting is a two-site edit that nothing checks: exactly one of
       the two packages ever runs on a given machine, so an omission from the x86 list ships as "that setting
       silently does nothing on 32-bit hosts", and CI runs the bundle only on a 64-bit runner.

       The list of names lives in Bundle.wxs (JasForwardedSettings), next to the Variable declarations it has
       to agree with.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- The architecture policy, in one line: whichever package matches the machine may replace an installed
       product of the other architecture. The MSI's own gate stays strict for a bare .msi run by hand. -->
  <MsiProperty Name="FORCE_UPGRADE" Value="1" />

  <!-- Each setting is forwarded verbatim from the like-named bundle variable. All default to empty, so an
       unset one reaches the MSI as an empty property and the MSI's own default applies - the bundle restates
       none of those defaults, since two copies of a default value is how they drift apart. -->
  <?foreach JasSetting in $(JasForwardedSettings) ?>
    <MsiProperty Name="$(JasSetting)" Value="[$(JasSetting)]" />
  <?endforeach?>

</Include>
